    id: "ch-processes", kind: "chapter", page: "processes",
    name: "How rugs are made, and where tufting fits",
    formerNames: ["How rugs are made: a short history of the processes"],
    intro: "Draft (built from the evidence below; rewrite freely):\n\nEvery rug is one of three things: a cloth woven or knotted with its pile in it, a finished cloth with pile added afterwards, or a mat made without yarn construction at all. Tufting belongs to the second family. Knowing the others is the only way to see what it changed.\n\nThe oldest family needs no loom. Felt is wool matted together by heat, moisture and friction, with no yarn and no adhesive. Britannica notes that non-woven felts are considered among the earliest fabrics; the firm evidence is the decorated felt appliqué hangings from the Pazyryk burial mounds in the Altai (5th–3rd century BCE), now in the Hermitage. The technique lives on: Turkmen weavers still lay dyed wool on a reed mat, wet it, roll it and kick the roll (a 2007 example is in Cooper Hewitt), and Kyrgyz shyrdak makers cut two felts in counterchange and sew them together, a craft UNESCO listed in 2012 (British Museum As1995,20.14).\n\nFlatweaves have no pile. In tapestry weave each coloured weft runs only where its colour is needed and is packed down to hide the warp; the earliest known tapestry is linen from the tomb of Thutmose IV in Egypt (about 1483–1411 BCE). Kilims are tapestry rugs: where colours meet, the weaver leaves a slit, dovetails, or interlocks the wefts (a 17th-century slit-tapestry kilim is in San Francisco). Soumak wraps coloured wefts around the warps instead, so the face looks almost embroidered (Caucasus, 1875–1925, Textile Museum). Navajo weavers worked tapestry on an upright loom with no moving parts; sources place the start anywhere from about 1600 to after the 1680 Pueblo Revolt, and floor rugs for outside buyers date from about 1880. In India the cotton dhurrie is beaten down with a pronged panja. Sweden's rölakan, reversible and interlocked, is so far known only from dealers' descriptions.\n\nKnotted pile is the luxury standard. The weaver ties short lengths of yarn around pairs of warps, row by row, cuts them to height and locks each row with wefts. The Pazyryk carpet, the oldest known pile carpet, is all wool with symmetric knots; radiocarbon dates it to the 5th–4th century BCE, though the Hermitage's listing dates the barrow to 252–238 BCE. Knot types identify traditions: the symmetric \"Turkish\" knot, the asymmetric \"Persian\" knot that allows finer curves, the single-warp \"Spanish\" knot (an armorial carpet of 1415–58 in the Textile Museum), and the Tibetan knot, in which the yarn is looped over a gauge rod and cut with a knife run along the rod. Scandinavian rya, long-piled and first used on beds, is documented from the mid-1400s (a Finnish ryijy dated 1814 is in the V&A). Nomads knot on horizontal ground looms they can take down at each camp; villages and workshops use vertical looms.\n\nThen pile moved onto the loom (chapter 3). Brussels and Wilton raised pile over wires. Two Scottish inventions cut the cost of colour: Richard Whytock's \"tapestry\" carpet printed the pattern onto the pile warp before weaving (patented 1832; another source says about 1840), and James Templeton and William Quiglay patented chenille Axminster in 1839, weaving a patterned blanket, cutting it into furry strips and weaving those in as pile. Skinner's spool Axminster (1876) followed.\n\nThe second family adds pile to a cloth that already exists. Yarn-sewn rugs use the bed rug's running stitch through woven cloth, leaving loops that are usually clipped (a Connecticut hearth rug of 1800–1820). Shirred rugs gather strips of cloth into caterpillar strands and sew them flat (Historic New England, 1820–30; sources date the fashion 1820–1850, one about 1870). Hooking pulls strips up through a foundation from the front with a hook. Where it began is debated: W. W. Kent's The Hooked Rug (1930) traced it to Yorkshire, but Colonial Williamsburg's label places its start in New England and south-eastern Canada in the 1840s, and it spread once cheap burlap arrived from the 1850s; the oldest dated Canadian hooked rug is from 1860. In Labrador and Newfoundland, the Grenfell Mission turned hooking into a cottage industry (founded 1906, 1909 or 1913 by different accounts), asking supporters in 1928 for worn silk stockings to hook. Braided rugs coil a three-strand plait of wool strips (1860–1890 in Historic New England's example), rag rugs weave strips as weft on a loom (popular in the US from the 1830s, says the Shaker Museum), and crocheted rugs loop strips with a hook into a mat with no foundation at all; one in the American Folk Art Museum reuses old ingrain carpet.\n\nTufting is the industrial form of that second family: needles push pile yarn through a backing that already exists, and latex or another adhesive locks it in. Britannica's student encyclopedia names weaving and tufting as today's two main methods. What tufting removed was the slowest step. A loom builds cloth and pile together, and knotting ties every tuft, but a bar of hundreds of needles can loop a whole row in one stroke into cloth made elsewhere. The rest of this book follows that idea from the bed rug's needle to the robot.\n\nIndustry then found ways to skip yarn construction altogether. Needle-punch mats a web of fibres with barbed needles (padding from the late 1800s, synthetic floor coverings from the late 1950s). Heuga in the Netherlands cut bitumen-backed needlefelt into carpet tiles around 1956–58. Flocked flooring (Flotex: France in the 1960s, Britain from 1971) shoots short nylon fibres upright into adhesive in an electric field. Fusion-bonded carpet sets cut yarn into PVC (Debron of Kidderminster; Milliken took the US rights in 1969). Printing moved pattern off the loom entirely: flat-screen carpet printing from 1962 and rotary screens from 1968 (the maker's dates), then computer-controlled jet dyeing in the 1970s. Knitted carpet, which loops pile and backing together in one operation, exists but has no dated beginning in the sources found.\n\nStill to find:\n- Hermitage inventory numbers for the Pazyryk carpet and felts, and a settled date.\n- A museum-dated braided rug before 1860; a dated punch-needle tool besides Ross's 1886 punch hook (chapter 9).\n- Museum or scholarly sources for rölakan and trasmatta (dealer sources only so far).\n- Who first made knitted carpet, and when; the year fusion bonding was invented.\n- Winterthur examples of each process (yarn-sewn, shirred, hooked, braided) for the fellowship application.",
