    id: "ch-before-machine", kind: "chapter", page: "before-the-machine",
    name: "Before the machine: bed rugs and needle-made pile",
    formerNames: ["Before the machine: needle-made pile in early America"],
    intro: "Draft (built from the evidence below; rewrite freely):\n\nTufting did not begin with a machine. It began with a needle, a length of wool and a woven ground. From the eighteenth century (the standard 1972 survey is titled Bed Ruggs/1722–1833), women in New England made bed rugs: heavy bedcovers worked by pushing wool through a wool or linen ground and leaving loops on the surface, sometimes cut into pile. The Metropolitan Museum's notes on its 1809 bed rug make the key point: the pile is needleworked, not hooked, a distinction once misunderstood. That makes these objects direct technical ancestors of hand tufting.\n\nWinterthur's bed rug inscribed \"Mary Foot AD 1778\" anchors the story. Laurel Thatcher Ulrich, in The Age of Homespun, links it to an \"E.F.\" rug at the Connecticut Historical Society and an unmarked blue-and-white rug at Historic Deerfield. All three may mark the Foot siblings' marriages of 5 November 1778. Dated and named pieces follow: Esther Packard (1801), a rug marked \"C/1819\", the Met's 1796 and 1809 rugs. Together they let a reader see a domestic technique, not a trade.\n\nThe technique matters more than the name. A bed rug was sewn: the maker drew yarn down and up through a foundation with a needle, leaving a loop on the face each time, and on many rugs cut the loops afterwards. That is the hand version of what a tufting machine does: a needle carries yarn through a backing, something holds the loop, and a knife may cut it. The bed rugs' designs point outward too. The blue-and-white rug at Historic Deerfield shares its Tree of Life with the Met's 1796 bed rug, an Indian motif.\n\nIn the early nineteenth century the needle moved to cotton. Candlewick spreads (roughly 1800–1830 in the examples here, with dated pieces of 1820, 1824 and 1825) combined flat stitches with raised ones, tufting among them. Museum notes say these spreads recall the earlier yarn-sewn bed rugs. The Caswell carpet (1832–35) shows the same hand at floor scale, though the Met questions how much of its cloth and yarn Zeruah Caswell made herself.\n\nFloor rugs came later and in several forms. The 2007 exhibition \"The Great Cover-Up\" grouped yarn-sewn, shirred, appliquéd and embroidered rugs as early American work for beds, tables and floors, and the Caswell carpet even has a detachable hearth section. Hooked rugs, made by pulling loops up through a foundation with a hook from the front, became the common homemade floor rug in the nineteenth century; Winterthur holds examples catalogued from the mid-1800s, and the Laura M. Allen scrapbook in its library follows their 1920s revival. Hooking and needle-sewing are different motions, but both make pile by hand from a continuous yarn, and the hand tools of chapter 9 grew out of that world.\n\nThe argument of the chapter: the hand technique that Dalton later revived, and machines later copied, was already old in America. It was domestic, female and made for the bed before the floor.\n\nStill to find:\n- Which of Winterthur's bed rugs have cut rather than looped pile (check with the textile curator).\n- Any tufted work between the candlewick spreads and Catherine Evans (c. 1850–1890). One lead: the Index of American Design's \"Shaker Tufted Wool Rug\" (Kentucky; watercolour by Orville A. Carroll, c. 1938). The rug's own date, and what \"tufted\" means on it, are not known yet.\n- The Bed Ruggs (1972) checklist, for Winterthur loans.\n- Winterthur's hooked rugs: accession numbers and dates, for a short catalogue in the fellowship application.",
