    id: "ch-machine", kind: "chapter", page: "machine",
    name: "Building the machine",
    intro: "Draft (built from the evidence below; rewrite freely):\n\nThe tufting machine was built from the sewing machine, not from the hand tool. The earliest filings in this database are sewing-machine patents. William Barnett's 1920 \"Sewing Machine\" says it \"may be more specifically termed a tufting machine\". August Carter of Chattanooga filed in 1922 for a tufting attachment with a needle, a loop former and a separate cutting knife: the combination every later machine uses.\n\nEvery tufting machine does three things: a needle carries yarn through a backing, a looper catches and holds the loop as the needle rises, and, for cut pile, a knife cuts the loop. Carter's 1922 patent is the first in this database to claim all three on one machine. The hand tools of chapter 9 did the same with two hands; the machine did it with a shaft and cams, many times a second.\n\nThrough the 1920s and 30s inventors in Chattanooga, Dalton and the Northeast worked the problem from several sides. John Gladish filed five patents between 1927 and 1934, some for rug tufting at Valway Rug Mills. Harry Dykeman (1928) and Walter Loos (1932, 1935) built rug tufting machines. Moench's 1928 single-needle carpet tufting apparatus, later patented in a multi-needle version, was named an ASME Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark in 2018. In Dalton, Glenn Looper adapted a Singer sewing machine (he filed in 1936; granted 1938), and Roy Windham and Jess Kile followed in 1939. By 1941 Windham had also written a manual for machine fixers, The Tufting Machine, then in its third edition (Rome, Georgia), which suggests earlier editions in the 1930s. The museum line that \"the first tufting machine was built in 1930\" has no patent behind it. \"Dalton, the 1930s\" is safer.\n\nThe decisive step was multiplying the needles. Joe Cobble's \"Multiple Needle Tufting Machine\" (filed 1941, granted 1943), credited locally to Cobble and George Muse, is seen as the foundation of modern carpet tufting. Lyman Hamrick (1944) and Walter Hamby (1949) followed, and in the 1950s Joseph Card added thread feed, pattern control and loop shedding (1955–58). Widened from bedspreads to broadloom, these machines turned a 10% share of US carpet in 1950 into more than 90% today.\n\nPatterns came next. Joseph Card's patents of 1955–58 controlled yarn feed and pattern so that pile height could change across the carpet, and his patterned-tufting apparatus (filed 1956) was also filed in Britain in 1957. From then on the machine's story is as much about control as about needles: chain-driven roll and scroll attachments, then servos and computers (chapter 12).\n\nThe builders became an industry of their own around Chattanooga: Cobble Brothers (from the 1930s; Singer-Cobble by 1960; Vandewiele 2013), Tuftco (1960, as Southern Machine Company), and Card-Monroe (1981), into which Lewis and Roy Card, the Cobbles' nephews, bought in 1983. British patents filed by Cobble and Singer-Cobble at Blackburn (1957–65) show the machine being developed abroad as well as exported. Groz-Beckert in Germany supplies the needles. Today, by the industry's own figure, more than 70% of the world's carpet (about 85% of America's) is made within about an hour of Dalton. Geographers have asked why it clustered there (McGregor and Maxey, 1974; Walters and Wheeler, 1984).\n\nThe machine's history also complicates a national story. German sources credit the machine to the United States and Britain, and German firms tufted from the 1950s without building broadloom machines, though Girmes of Krefeld patented a plush tufting machine around 1971 and Germany's Groz-Beckert supplies the needles (tufting needles since 1998).\n\nStill to find:\n- The joint Cobble–Muse patent, if separate from Joe Cobble's 1941 filing.\n- Glenn Dalton's 1936 filing named on the historical marker (not found).\n- Which machine first tufted broadloom width, and when (c. 1950).\n- Photographs of early Dalton and Chattanooga machines (Bandy Heritage Center; ASME landmark file for Moench).\n- A copy of Windham's The Tufting Machine (3rd ed., 1941) and any earlier edition: none found online (try the Bandy Heritage Center and Rome, Georgia libraries).\n- DuPont's \"Chronology of ITP tufting (weaving)\", 3 July 1962 (Hagley, Acc. 2632).",
    items: ["tool-moench-1928", "co-cobble", "co-tuftco", "co-card-monroe", "co-groz-beckert", "pat-gb853746a", "pat-gb880860a", "pat-gb821702a", "pat-gb1067502a", "pat-de2621360a1", "pat-de1785451c", "event-looper-tufting-machine", "marker-mechanization-bedspread", "patent-us2977905", "book-deaton-bedspreads-broadloom", "book-patton-carpet-capital", "article-patton-ehnet", "ttw-carter-1922", "ttw-first-machine-1930-refuted", "ttw-tufted-share", "ttw-dalton-share", "chenille-widened", "pat-us1452632a", "pat-us1485213a", "pat-us1800522a", "pat-us2042503a", "pat-us1831485a", "pat-us1907292a", "pat-us1909530a", "pat-us1909531a", "pat-us2224866a", "pat-us2123114a", "pat-us2311802a", "pat-us2389914a", "pat-us2335487a", "pat-us2411267a", "pat-us2595355a", "pat-us2935037a", "pat-us2862465a", "pat-us2975736a", "pat-us1970703a", "pat-us2070287a", "book-windham-tufting-machine-1941", "article-mcgregor-maxey-1974", "article-walters-wheeler-1984", "book-reynolds-innovation-1968", "u-mvs3rm86ifn5j"],
    todo: ["Moench's patent: its number, and whether it dates from 1928 or 1934.", "The first broadloom-width tufting machine and its date; the date of the Bandy Heritage Center photograph of the 216-inch Cobble machine.", "Filing dates for the patents known so far only by year of grant (Smith 1933, Maples and Weaver 1934, Warren 1936, Windham 1938, Bills and Patey 1944, Jones 1954), and the \"Glenn Dalton\" filing of 1936 named on the Dalton historical marker, which has not been found. (marker-mechanization-bedspread)", "A copy of Windham's *The Tufting Machine* (third edition, 1941) or an earlier edition (Bandy Heritage Center; Rome, Georgia, libraries).", "Whether Joseph Lewis Card, inventor of the 1955–58 patents, is Lewis Card Sr. of Tuftco and Card-Monroe.", "When Cobble's Blackburn plant began: 1955 (Grace's Guide) or 1956 (Albert Mill).", "DuPont's \"Chronology of ITP tufting (weaving)\", 3 July 1962, at Hagley (Acc. 2632), and what \"ITP\" means. (u-mvs3rm86ifn5j)", "Sources for the 10 and 90 per cent tufted shares and for the 70 and 85 per cent made near Dalton."]
